clamp the step the animations are integrated over

The shelf settles on a critically damped spring stepped by the real frame
time (`Shelf::update`, OMEGA = 16):

    vel += (-2w*vel - w*w*(scroll - target)) * dt
    scroll += vel * dt

The velocity term is `vel * (1 - 2w*dt)`, which turns negative past
dt = 1/2w = 31.25 ms: the velocity flips sign every frame and the carousel
rings back and forth instead of settling. It was reported from the handheld as
"it goes back and forth rapidly before it stops", and it was reported as
happening on the Game Boy Color shelf and not the Game Boy one, which is the
tell: a clear shell is the most expensive cart to draw and three of them in
view is what pushed the frame over the line.

Measured on the device the same evening, scrolling that run of three:

    soft: clear 0.7 sprites 25.6-37.3 over 18 draws, out 3.0 ms/frame, worst 75.3

30-41 ms a frame, either side of 31.25.

Clamping the step restores what the spring is for rather than changing it:
critical damping, no oscillation. An animation on a frame slower than the
clamp runs slow rather than wrong, which is the right way round. 25 ms leaves
the velocity term at 0.2 rather than near zero, so it damps hard instead of
creeping up on the flip.

OMEGA is public now because the clamp is derived from it, and the two tests
check the derivation rather than the number.

video_mode.rs is a one-line test import, not part of this: `OUT_W` was left
unimported for the test module when 0009 dropped it from the file's own uses,
and nothing compiled the test binary until this patch added a test to it.

